/// carried borrow, least-significant first, the same order encode already
/// writes in. Each step reads three bits (a's, b's, the incoming borrow)
/// and produces a result bit and an outgoing borrow, the same three-input
/// truth table a hardware subtractor uses -- there is no `-` anywhere in
/// this function. A borrow surviving past the last bit means a < b, which
/// this unsigned word format cannot hold, so that case is None rather than
/// a wrapped or assumed answer.
pub fn subtract_via_word(a: &BigUint, b: &BigUint) -> Option<BigUint> {
