1	// winding_period.rs — winding-period engine, native Rust, torus by default.
2	//
3	// The cyclic subgroup <a> mod N is a torus: the exponent is a winding
4	// coordinate x mod r, and a^x sits at winding x/r on the unit circle.
5	// The period r is the winding number at which the loop closes — a^r = 1,
6	// the discrete ∮A = 2πn with n = r. BSGS walks the torus from both sides
7	// of its diameter: baby steps one radius clockwise, giant steps the other
8	// radius back; they meet at the winding midpoint. minimal_winding strips
9	// the period to its reduced denominator; closure_gcd closes p at bound B
10	// exactly when max_prime_power(ord_p(2)) ≤ B (the sharpened threshold
11	// theorem, 1156/1156 — the engine closes at the ORDER, not at p−1).
12	//
13	// no_std + alloc: the baby walk lands in a sorted (value, exponent) lattice
14	// and the giant walk binary-searches it — no HashMap dependency, and the
15	// lattice IS the torus quantization.
