3	//! The artifact "Trilattice-native factorization" is a verified ob3ect whose
4	//! glyph word `⊢≻⊙∈⊤⋈⊥≺⊞⊡∋⋈⊣` grounds each of the twelve marks to a role in
5	//! factoring a number:
6	//!
7	//!   ⊢ VINIT   void_bulk                the empty register before scale-collapse
8	//!   ≻ AFWD    scale_ascent             one forward step up the number line
9	//!   ⊙ IMSCRIB critical_phase           the self-reference checkpoint
10	//!   ∈ FSPLIT  asymmetric_factorization the fork: n splits into two unequal poles
11	//!   ⊤ EVALT   kinetic_freeze           the frozen pole, held on the fork's T arm
12	//!   ⋈ CLINK   fidelity_chain           carry
13	//!   ⊥ EVALF   polarity_interface       the moving pole, held on the fork's F arm
14	//!   ≺ AREV    chiral_descent           the descent that clears the open register
15	//!   ⊞ ENGAGR  transition_stabilization the both-state holding the accumulated pair
16	//!   ⊡ IFIX    cluster_propagation      the winding commit, the one fixation
17	//!   ∋ FFUSE   winding_reconstitution   the fuse: the two poles rejoin as a factor
18	//!   ⋈ CLINK   fidelity_chain           carry
19	//!   ⊣ TANCH   hierarchical_container   the closed boundary
20	//!
21	//! What this tool reads off the word, and what it does not.
22	//!
23	//! The reading is real and checkable. Run the word as a ring and its landing
24	//! register is `tf` at every cut but one; the single exception is the cut led
25	//! by the winding fixation ⊡, which commits to a clean `T`. That single-commit
26	//! cut is the ob3ect's cluster_propagation event, and it survives the control:
27	//! scrambling the marks smears the commit across several cuts, so the
28	//! one-commit-per-winding reading is in the word's arrangement, not the marks.
29	//! The fork ∈ (asymmetric_factorization) banks its two poles, the T pole
30	//! (kinetic_freeze) and the F pole (polarity_interface), so they survive the
31	//! chiral descent ≺ that clears the open register, and fuse at ∋
32	//! (winding_reconstitution). That is the factor pair held across the descent,
33	//! read straight from `weight` / `cycle`.
34	//!
35	//! The divisor search is not the word. As `prime_winding` establishes at
36	//! length against concrete counterexamples, closure alone never produces a
37	//! divisor: a bounded per-mark reading sees a bounded pattern in the digits,
38	//! and a factor is a global fact about divisibility. So the actual factoring
39	//! here IS `prime_winding`'s search, called directly, never re-copied. This
40	//! module carries the trilattice reading of the number and of the word; the
41	//! arithmetic that splits n lives one place, next door.
42	//!
43	//! The winding route closes part of that gap. `winding` reads the factor off a
44	//! real winding: the multiplicative order r of a base a mod n, the ROTAT period
45	//! of the ring a generates, from which gcd(a^(r/2) ± 1, n) splits n. That is
46	//! Shor's classical core, native to the SIC-phase structure this codebase runs
47	//! on (belnap_phase_shor), and it is the factor read from a winding rather than
48	//! from a rho search. It runs at arbitrary precision, carrying the full residue
49	//! at every step. The order search is the table-free leaping search of the
50	//! ob3ect `table_free_winding_by_leaping`: two leapers cross the ring in large
51	//! pseudo-random jumps and are known by where their paths meet, the ∈ fork
52	//! setting them and the ∋ fuse resolving their crossing, so the winding comes
53	//! back in about sqrt(order) leaps holding only the two leapers, no table. The
54	//! large jump is load-bearing: a small uniform stride would drift a full order
55	//! instead of meeting early. There is no memory cap; the only limit is a leap-
56	//! step time budget.
57	//!
58	//! The number and the winding both enter the Grammar's own marks now. `read`
59	//! shows n's native parity-graded word (Native IMASM-Numeral Mapping), the
60	//! bijective encoding, beside the hex one; and when the winding route finds an
61	//! order r, it records r as its own native numeral, the ⊡ immutable_record of
62	//! the winding value.
63	//!
64	//! The rung once standing here, closed: parity, the Z2 grade the native word
65	//! carries, does not determine the order by itself — many powers a^k mod n
66	//! share a parity, so on n=19,a=8 (order 6) and n=61,a=21 (order 12) the
67	//! parity sequence closes on a shorter cycle (2 and 4) than the true order.
68	//! `trilattice_factor dialect-probe` swept all 88 cross-dialect gates against
69	//! the same residues and found DIALECT_REGISTER: 12 dialects whose gate-pass
70	//! sequence reached the exact true order on all four cases checked (those two
71	//! plus n=143,a=3 and n=91,a=2), where parity fell short on the first two.
72	//! `read` now reports that register alongside the parity word. It replaces
73	//! parity's wrong answer with a correct one; it is not a faster one — each
74	//! gated axis is a fixed 3-to-5-value enum (imas_ig::IgPrim), so no single
75	//! reading, or handful, can carry an order of arbitrary size. Recovering the
76	//! order still costs observing enough of the register's own period, same as
77	//! it did with parity, just now landing on the right period instead of a
78	//! spurious short one.
79	//!
80	//! Subcommands:
81	//!   trilattice_factor word            the canonical glyph word and its marks
82	//!   trilattice_factor cert            the winding-commit certificate of the word
83	//!   trilattice_factor read <n>        the trilattice reading of n's digit word
84	//!   trilattice_factor winding <n> [a] factor n off a multiplicative-order winding
85	//!   trilattice_factor factor <n>      factor n (arbitrary precision) with the reading
86	//!   trilattice_factor dialect-probe <n> <a> [max_k]  gate-pass period vs. parity's, over a^k mod n
87	//!   trilattice_factor help            list subcommands
